WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.984 align:middle line:90%


00:00:00.984 --> 00:00:07.380 align:middle line:84%
[SQUEAKING]
[RUSTLING] [CLICKING]

00:00:07.380 --> 00:00:11.670 align:middle line:90%


00:00:11.670 --> 00:00:14.900 align:middle line:84%
VASILY STRELA: So today's
class is introductory class.

00:00:14.900 --> 00:00:18.340 align:middle line:84%
We'll talk about
the class itself

00:00:18.340 --> 00:00:22.560 align:middle line:84%
and about the
program of the class.

00:00:22.560 --> 00:00:31.680 align:middle line:84%
Then Jake will talk a little
bit about the financial terms

00:00:31.680 --> 00:00:34.260 align:middle line:90%
and ideas.

00:00:34.260 --> 00:00:37.920 align:middle line:84%
And I'll talk about
bond math a little bit.

00:00:37.920 --> 00:00:47.360 align:middle line:84%
So the class, because it is
organized to introduce you

00:00:47.360 --> 00:00:54.480 align:middle line:84%
to the applications of
math in the real world,

00:00:54.480 --> 00:00:58.640 align:middle line:84%
the class is split between
a mathematical part, which

00:00:58.640 --> 00:01:03.000 align:middle line:84%
will be taught by Peter,
and application part, which

00:01:03.000 --> 00:01:08.400 align:middle line:84%
will be taught by many people
who come from industry.

00:01:08.400 --> 00:01:12.440 align:middle line:84%
Maybe start with a little
bit of introduction of myself

00:01:12.440 --> 00:01:15.600 align:middle line:90%
and how it all comes together.

00:01:15.600 --> 00:01:20.680 align:middle line:90%
So I was at MIT a long time ago.

00:01:20.680 --> 00:01:26.040 align:middle line:84%
I got my PhD from math
department in '92.

00:01:26.040 --> 00:01:29.280 align:middle line:90%
My advisor was Gil Strang.

00:01:29.280 --> 00:01:37.920 align:middle line:84%
And my topic of my PhD had
nothing to do with finance.

00:01:37.920 --> 00:01:39.760 align:middle line:84%
It was actually
applications where

00:01:39.760 --> 00:01:42.280 align:middle line:84%
it was about wavelets
and applications

00:01:42.280 --> 00:01:44.320 align:middle line:90%
were in signal processing.

00:01:44.320 --> 00:01:50.520 align:middle line:84%
So after that, I was a math
professor for a few years

00:01:50.520 --> 00:01:52.800 align:middle line:90%
and did math research.

00:01:52.800 --> 00:01:56.960 align:middle line:84%
But at some point, I decided
to switch to the industry.

00:01:56.960 --> 00:02:02.020 align:middle line:84%
And somehow I did like
mathematical finance.

00:02:02.020 --> 00:02:04.920 align:middle line:84%
So since then, I've
been a quant working

00:02:04.920 --> 00:02:12.140 align:middle line:84%
in mathematical finance, worked
in many places for a long time.

00:02:12.140 --> 00:02:15.240 align:middle line:84%
I was with Morgan
Stanley, and right now, I

00:02:15.240 --> 00:02:17.770 align:middle line:90%
run fixed-income quants at RBC.

00:02:17.770 --> 00:02:31.640 align:middle line:90%


00:02:31.640 --> 00:02:35.540 align:middle line:84%
I very much appreciate
having experience,

00:02:35.540 --> 00:02:38.260 align:middle line:84%
both with academia
and with industry.

00:02:38.260 --> 00:02:46.100 align:middle line:84%
And that's what I would like
to show you through the class.

00:02:46.100 --> 00:02:49.600 align:middle line:84%
So now a little bit
more about the class.

00:02:49.600 --> 00:02:52.080 align:middle line:84%
So as I said, there
will be math part,

00:02:52.080 --> 00:02:54.020 align:middle line:90%
and there will be finance part.

00:02:54.020 --> 00:02:57.320 align:middle line:84%
The lectures will
be intertwined, not

00:02:57.320 --> 00:03:03.920 align:middle line:84%
necessarily one after
another but more or less

00:03:03.920 --> 00:03:05.580 align:middle line:90%
in equal proportion.

00:03:05.580 --> 00:03:09.120 align:middle line:90%


00:03:09.120 --> 00:03:12.240 align:middle line:84%
In terms of prerequisites,
I do think that you all

00:03:12.240 --> 00:03:16.120 align:middle line:84%
have necessary mathematical
prerequisites, which

00:03:16.120 --> 00:03:21.600 align:middle line:84%
is linear algebra, some
statistics, and probably

00:03:21.600 --> 00:03:25.760 align:middle line:84%
some calculus and
mathematical analysis.

00:03:25.760 --> 00:03:30.920 align:middle line:84%
We don't require any prior
knowledge of finance,

00:03:30.920 --> 00:03:38.560 align:middle line:84%
even though if you have been
doing some work or internships

00:03:38.560 --> 00:03:42.000 align:middle line:90%
before, that certainly helps.

00:03:42.000 --> 00:03:46.700 align:middle line:84%
Here is the schedule,
which we intend to keep,

00:03:46.700 --> 00:03:51.640 align:middle line:84%
and please keep in mind that
things may change and may shift,

00:03:51.640 --> 00:03:55.800 align:middle line:84%
particularly with
invited speakers.

00:03:55.800 --> 00:04:00.920 align:middle line:84%
Peter will talk about the
mathematical part here.

00:04:00.920 --> 00:04:05.140 align:middle line:84%
I'll just introduce
a few classes.

00:04:05.140 --> 00:04:09.720 align:middle line:84%
So in a week's time,
Jeff Shen from BlackRock

00:04:09.720 --> 00:04:12.640 align:middle line:84%
will talk about
equity investing,

00:04:12.640 --> 00:04:16.360 align:middle line:84%
in particular, quantitative
equity investing.

00:04:16.360 --> 00:04:20.320 align:middle line:84%
Then Stefan Andreev
from Two Sigma

00:04:20.320 --> 00:04:24.020 align:middle line:84%
will talk about principal
component analysis and finance,

00:04:24.020 --> 00:04:28.600 align:middle line:84%
which should go well
together with what Peter will

00:04:28.600 --> 00:04:31.840 align:middle line:90%
be talking about at the time.

00:04:31.840 --> 00:04:36.560 align:middle line:84%
Then Andrew
Gustavsson from Mizuho

00:04:36.560 --> 00:04:41.940 align:middle line:84%
will talk about linear
rates, product swaps, bonds,

00:04:41.940 --> 00:04:44.760 align:middle line:90%
and curve construction.

00:04:44.760 --> 00:04:48.540 align:middle line:84%
Then James Sheppard from
Quantile Technologies,

00:04:48.540 --> 00:04:51.880 align:middle line:84%
which is now part of
London Stock Exchange,

00:04:51.880 --> 00:04:56.240 align:middle line:84%
will talk about
optimization and compressing

00:04:56.240 --> 00:04:59.600 align:middle line:90%
derivative portfolios.

00:04:59.600 --> 00:05:10.200 align:middle line:84%
Then a very interesting lecture
by Tarek Mansoor and Luna Lopez

00:05:10.200 --> 00:05:12.160 align:middle line:90%
from Kalshi.

00:05:12.160 --> 00:05:14.700 align:middle line:84%
They'll talk about their
company and how they started.

00:05:14.700 --> 00:05:21.880 align:middle line:84%
They both are MIT alums and
actually alums from the class,

00:05:21.880 --> 00:05:24.400 align:middle line:90%
from a few years ago.

00:05:24.400 --> 00:05:28.440 align:middle line:84%
Then Ross Garon
from Millennium will

00:05:28.440 --> 00:05:36.760 align:middle line:84%
talk about investments and
systematic investments.

00:05:36.760 --> 00:05:39.840 align:middle line:84%
Andrew Lo from MIT
will talk actually

00:05:39.840 --> 00:05:45.040 align:middle line:84%
about applying some techniques
from computational finance

00:05:45.040 --> 00:05:51.040 align:middle line:84%
into biomedicine and
biomedical portfolio.

00:05:51.040 --> 00:05:56.100 align:middle line:84%
Oh, I missed both
Jake and myself there,

00:05:56.100 --> 00:06:00.040 align:middle line:84%
but Jake will talk about
portfolio optimization,

00:06:00.040 --> 00:06:02.960 align:middle line:84%
and I'll talk about
Black-Scholes.

00:06:02.960 --> 00:06:04.800 align:middle line:84%
And the last
invited lecture will

00:06:04.800 --> 00:06:11.300 align:middle line:84%
be by John Hull, who is
legendary in derivatives space,

00:06:11.300 --> 00:06:17.200 align:middle line:84%
but he will actually talk about
machine learning, his newest

00:06:17.200 --> 00:06:17.800 align:middle line:90%
interest.

00:06:17.800 --> 00:06:22.840 align:middle line:84%
With that, let me
pass it on to Peter.

00:06:22.840 --> 00:06:26.280 align:middle line:90%
PETER: OK, thank you, Vasily.

00:06:26.280 --> 00:06:31.120 align:middle line:84%
Let me just page back up to
the schedule of lectures.

00:06:31.120 --> 00:06:34.580 align:middle line:84%
Although, before that, let me
give a brief self-introduction.

00:06:34.580 --> 00:06:37.500 align:middle line:84%
I have a PhD degree
in statistics,

00:06:37.500 --> 00:06:39.840 align:middle line:84%
and this was a
mathematical statistics

00:06:39.840 --> 00:06:42.040 align:middle line:90%
program at UC Berkeley.

00:06:42.040 --> 00:06:46.560 align:middle line:84%
And I was getting
rather distracted

00:06:46.560 --> 00:06:50.520 align:middle line:84%
with mathematical
arguments and analysis

00:06:50.520 --> 00:06:53.280 align:middle line:84%
and took a class at
the business school

00:06:53.280 --> 00:06:56.600 align:middle line:84%
at Berkeley, the
Haas School, and that

00:06:56.600 --> 00:07:01.440 align:middle line:84%
renewed my passion, basically,
for studying finance

00:07:01.440 --> 00:07:04.000 align:middle line:90%
and quantitative finance.

00:07:04.000 --> 00:07:07.720 align:middle line:84%
I taught in the statistics
department at Harvard

00:07:07.720 --> 00:07:10.240 align:middle line:84%
and then joined
the Sloan School,

00:07:10.240 --> 00:07:14.560 align:middle line:84%
where I pursued research
on statistical modeling

00:07:14.560 --> 00:07:16.560 align:middle line:90%
and finance.

00:07:16.560 --> 00:07:20.320 align:middle line:84%
That led to interesting
consulting projects

00:07:20.320 --> 00:07:23.980 align:middle line:90%
where one client hired--

00:07:23.980 --> 00:07:26.560 align:middle line:84%
well, wanted to hire
me to work on a trading

00:07:26.560 --> 00:07:29.340 align:middle line:84%
program in
international equities,

00:07:29.340 --> 00:07:33.440 align:middle line:84%
and we ended up agreeing
on becoming partners

00:07:33.440 --> 00:07:36.680 align:middle line:84%
rather than deciding how much
I should get paid for that.

00:07:36.680 --> 00:07:43.120 align:middle line:84%
And so I worked with hedge
funds for a number of years.

00:07:43.120 --> 00:07:45.320 align:middle line:84%
Ultimately had
the great pleasure

00:07:45.320 --> 00:07:51.820 align:middle line:84%
of working for IKOS, a
hedge fund in Cyprus,

00:07:51.820 --> 00:07:55.560 align:middle line:84%
which is in the
eastern Mediterranean.

00:07:55.560 --> 00:08:00.040 align:middle line:84%
Following that, I
came back to academics

00:08:00.040 --> 00:08:04.340 align:middle line:84%
actually to help with
this course, now, what?

00:08:04.340 --> 00:08:07.320 align:middle line:84%
11 years ago or
12 years ago, and

00:08:07.320 --> 00:08:10.760 align:middle line:84%
had the great pleasure to
work with Vasiliy and Jake

00:08:10.760 --> 00:08:12.580 align:middle line:90%
on this class.

00:08:12.580 --> 00:08:17.280 align:middle line:84%
And what I think is really
important to highlight,

00:08:17.280 --> 00:08:21.880 align:middle line:84%
is how the mathematics
lectures are teaching you

00:08:21.880 --> 00:08:25.720 align:middle line:84%
mathematics that's really
quite practical and useful.

00:08:25.720 --> 00:08:30.260 align:middle line:84%
So I love mathematics,
but I love,

00:08:30.260 --> 00:08:33.580 align:middle line:84%
I guess, financial
applications even more.

00:08:33.580 --> 00:08:36.600 align:middle line:84%
And so we'll be stressing
those tools that are

00:08:36.600 --> 00:08:41.159 align:middle line:90%
useful in financial modeling.

00:08:41.159 --> 00:08:47.560 align:middle line:84%
I do think it's important to
emphasize the stature of many

00:08:47.560 --> 00:08:49.480 align:middle line:90%
of these guest lectures.

00:08:49.480 --> 00:08:51.240 align:middle line:84%
And you're really
going to be hearing

00:08:51.240 --> 00:08:56.930 align:middle line:84%
from people who are the top in
the field, like John Hull, many

00:08:56.930 --> 00:09:02.730 align:middle line:84%
of you probably are familiar
with his work, and Andrew Lo.

00:09:02.730 --> 00:09:09.730 align:middle line:84%
And actually, I had the pleasure
of teaching Tariq and Luana

00:09:09.730 --> 00:09:13.470 align:middle line:84%
in statistics classes,
one of which was this.

00:09:13.470 --> 00:09:21.250 align:middle line:84%
And what they've done is
created a new futures exchange,

00:09:21.250 --> 00:09:25.250 align:middle line:90%
which is rather impressive.

00:09:25.250 --> 00:09:28.810 align:middle line:84%
When you look at the history of
the Commodity Futures Trading

00:09:28.810 --> 00:09:31.310 align:middle line:84%
Commission, there
may be, I don't know,

00:09:31.310 --> 00:09:35.970 align:middle line:84%
50 or so futures exchanges
that have come and gone,

00:09:35.970 --> 00:09:39.970 align:middle line:84%
maybe the most important, the
Chicago Mercantile Exchange.

00:09:39.970 --> 00:09:44.930 align:middle line:84%
But here, were two
students who basically

00:09:44.930 --> 00:09:48.710 align:middle line:84%
create an exchange where
you bet on discrete events,

00:09:48.710 --> 00:09:51.050 align:middle line:84%
whether things will
or will not occur.

00:09:51.050 --> 00:09:56.810 align:middle line:84%
And anyway, it's great to
have them join the class.

00:09:56.810 --> 00:09:58.410 align:middle line:90%
Well, let's see.

00:09:58.410 --> 00:10:01.090 align:middle line:84%
With the different
statistics or math lectures,

00:10:01.090 --> 00:10:04.290 align:middle line:90%
you can see the range of topics.

00:10:04.290 --> 00:10:08.290 align:middle line:84%
And these are all lectures
that introduce you

00:10:08.290 --> 00:10:10.550 align:middle line:84%
to these topics without
much background.

00:10:10.550 --> 00:10:13.170 align:middle line:84%
So I think it provides
an excellent introduction

00:10:13.170 --> 00:10:15.970 align:middle line:90%
to the different topics.

00:10:15.970 --> 00:10:19.770 align:middle line:84%
We will also be
using the language R

00:10:19.770 --> 00:10:22.790 align:middle line:90%
in our studio for illustrations.

00:10:22.790 --> 00:10:28.050 align:middle line:84%
And I think most students here
probably are Python experts.

00:10:28.050 --> 00:10:32.210 align:middle line:84%
I am not a Python
expert, so I use R.

00:10:32.210 --> 00:10:42.410 align:middle line:84%
But let me just tab over
to a slide showing this.

00:10:42.410 --> 00:10:43.930 align:middle line:90%
Let's see.

00:10:43.930 --> 00:10:48.450 align:middle line:84%
Using R, one of the reasons for
using R is that it gives you

00:10:48.450 --> 00:10:51.210 align:middle line:84%
immediate access to
state-of-the-art statistical

00:10:51.210 --> 00:10:53.010 align:middle line:90%
methods that get developed.

00:10:53.010 --> 00:10:57.530 align:middle line:84%
But in addition to that, it
facilitates the downloading

00:10:57.530 --> 00:11:01.330 align:middle line:84%
and collection of financial
data from the internet

00:11:01.330 --> 00:11:05.770 align:middle line:84%
or from other sources that
you might have access to.

00:11:05.770 --> 00:11:10.190 align:middle line:84%
But one can see issues like
nonlinear volatility models,

00:11:10.190 --> 00:11:15.370 align:middle line:84%
regime modeling those
are packages that

00:11:15.370 --> 00:11:19.450 align:middle line:90%
are quite useful and powerful.

00:11:19.450 --> 00:11:24.450 align:middle line:84%
In terms of sources of financial
data, everyone, I'm sure,

00:11:24.450 --> 00:11:27.490 align:middle line:84%
looks at the financial
markets a bit.

00:11:27.490 --> 00:11:34.530 align:middle line:84%
And any data that's available
on Yahoo, is importable into R.

00:11:34.530 --> 00:11:40.290 align:middle line:84%
Also, the Federal Reserve
Economic Database has all--

00:11:40.290 --> 00:11:45.290 align:middle line:84%
well, around 80,000 different
time series of different rates.

00:11:45.290 --> 00:11:47.350 align:middle line:90%
So let's see.

00:11:47.350 --> 00:11:51.770 align:middle line:84%
We distributed, in the
Canvas site. this R notebook,

00:11:51.770 --> 00:12:03.570 align:middle line:84%
FM Intro 1, which basically uses
the RStudio Cloud to collect

00:12:03.570 --> 00:12:05.410 align:middle line:90%
data and display it.

00:12:05.410 --> 00:12:09.850 align:middle line:84%
And so this year, we
have an alternative

00:12:09.850 --> 00:12:15.270 align:middle line:84%
to asking students to install
R desktop on their machines.

00:12:15.270 --> 00:12:20.030 align:middle line:84%
But just to sign up
to this RStudio Cloud,

00:12:20.030 --> 00:12:23.110 align:middle line:84%
and you can, basically
with a simple login,

00:12:23.110 --> 00:12:25.970 align:middle line:84%
create simple files
which we give you

00:12:25.970 --> 00:12:29.630 align:middle line:84%
and display different
time series.

00:12:29.630 --> 00:12:40.650 align:middle line:84%
So this FM Intro 1 R
program, basically,

00:12:40.650 --> 00:12:43.930 align:middle line:90%
will collect and display data.

00:12:43.930 --> 00:12:47.490 align:middle line:84%
And you don't need
to know anything,

00:12:47.490 --> 00:12:51.090 align:middle line:84%
except to maybe update the
dates for starting and ending

00:12:51.090 --> 00:12:52.410 align:middle line:90%
data collection.

00:12:52.410 --> 00:13:00.010 align:middle line:84%
And so one can look at the
S&P 500 index, the VIX index,

00:13:00.010 --> 00:13:02.930 align:middle line:84%
perhaps one of the
more important indexes

00:13:02.930 --> 00:13:07.970 align:middle line:84%
in equity markets is the VIX
index, sort of, a fear gauge.

00:13:07.970 --> 00:13:13.130 align:middle line:84%
And it turns out that
the fear gauge tends

00:13:13.130 --> 00:13:16.250 align:middle line:90%
to spike when the market drops.

00:13:16.250 --> 00:13:19.170 align:middle line:84%
And so there are
interesting relationships

00:13:19.170 --> 00:13:22.010 align:middle line:84%
between these that
actually get formalized

00:13:22.010 --> 00:13:24.690 align:middle line:90%
with option pricing theory.

00:13:24.690 --> 00:13:26.290 align:middle line:90%
And let's see.

00:13:26.290 --> 00:13:32.210 align:middle line:84%
Just for interest, here's
the time series for Bitcoin.

00:13:32.210 --> 00:13:34.130 align:middle line:84%
The beginning of
this time series

00:13:34.130 --> 00:13:36.850 align:middle line:90%
is about when our class began.

00:13:36.850 --> 00:13:43.610 align:middle line:84%
And if you were fortunate
to be at MIT at that time,

00:13:43.610 --> 00:13:48.750 align:middle line:84%
an alum of MIT gave all the
incoming students, I think,

00:13:48.750 --> 00:13:51.170 align:middle line:90%
$100 in Bitcoin.

00:13:51.170 --> 00:13:56.010 align:middle line:84%
And many years
later, some of them

00:13:56.010 --> 00:13:59.290 align:middle line:84%
still had their Bitcoin
keys and could cash in.

00:13:59.290 --> 00:14:02.170 align:middle line:84%
Some were very regretful
that they somehow

00:14:02.170 --> 00:14:04.450 align:middle line:90%
lost their Bitcoin keys.

00:14:04.450 --> 00:14:08.050 align:middle line:84%
But it's really an
interesting market.

00:14:08.050 --> 00:14:11.898 align:middle line:84%
I was a complete
skeptic back here.

00:14:11.898 --> 00:14:16.270 align:middle line:84%
[LAUGHS] And I guess I'm
still a complete skeptic.

00:14:16.270 --> 00:14:18.810 align:middle line:84%
But that being said,
there's obviously

00:14:18.810 --> 00:14:21.210 align:middle line:90%
a great potential there.

00:14:21.210 --> 00:14:25.970 align:middle line:84%
Here's the time series
graph of NVIDIA.

00:14:25.970 --> 00:14:30.690 align:middle line:84%
Curiously, in last year's
notes, we displayed NVIDIA.

00:14:30.690 --> 00:14:36.770 align:middle line:84%
And since then, [CHUCKLES]
it's risen rather dramatically.

00:14:36.770 --> 00:14:41.210 align:middle line:84%
A really interesting
topic for analysis

00:14:41.210 --> 00:14:46.290 align:middle line:84%
is the creation of bubbles
in financial markets,

00:14:46.290 --> 00:14:48.850 align:middle line:84%
and can you detect
a bubble and when it

00:14:48.850 --> 00:14:54.010 align:middle line:90%
might burst, very challenging.

00:14:54.010 --> 00:14:54.510 align:middle line:90%
Let's see.

00:14:54.510 --> 00:15:00.810 align:middle line:84%
One last point to illustrate
was, the crude oil futures

00:15:00.810 --> 00:15:03.330 align:middle line:90%
contract.

00:15:03.330 --> 00:15:08.010 align:middle line:84%
It's one of the more popular
futures contracts traded.

00:15:08.010 --> 00:15:14.530 align:middle line:84%
And what's rather
striking, is that in 2020,

00:15:14.530 --> 00:15:19.110 align:middle line:84%
the price of this contract
went negative, significantly.

00:15:19.110 --> 00:15:25.650 align:middle line:84%
And this was an event that the
brokers, important brokers,

00:15:25.650 --> 00:15:30.090 align:middle line:84%
didn't program in that
possibility into their systems.

00:15:30.090 --> 00:15:36.110 align:middle line:84%
And so when the system
became updated on this day

00:15:36.110 --> 00:15:39.650 align:middle line:84%
when the price was
significantly negative,

00:15:39.650 --> 00:15:46.450 align:middle line:84%
those who held, say, thousands
of contracts of the contract

00:15:46.450 --> 00:15:51.290 align:middle line:84%
actually owed money
to the exchange

00:15:51.290 --> 00:15:53.970 align:middle line:90%
for their negative position.

00:15:53.970 --> 00:15:57.210 align:middle line:84%
Anyway, the financial
markets offer these cases

00:15:57.210 --> 00:16:01.910 align:middle line:84%
where surprising, seemingly
impossible events can occur,

00:16:01.910 --> 00:16:03.730 align:middle line:90%
such as negative interest rates.

00:16:03.730 --> 00:16:06.850 align:middle line:84%
When I was studying
finance, the thought

00:16:06.850 --> 00:16:09.610 align:middle line:84%
of negative interest
rates was just

00:16:09.610 --> 00:16:12.570 align:middle line:90%
considered an impossibility.

00:16:12.570 --> 00:16:21.450 align:middle line:84%
So with that, let's see, I
just want to highlight RStudio.

00:16:21.450 --> 00:16:26.810 align:middle line:90%


00:16:26.810 --> 00:16:33.490 align:middle line:84%
Cloud allows you just to
log in with just a username

00:16:33.490 --> 00:16:35.090 align:middle line:90%
and password.

00:16:35.090 --> 00:16:36.370 align:middle line:90%
And it's free.

00:16:36.370 --> 00:16:40.490 align:middle line:84%
And then you can
create R programs,

00:16:40.490 --> 00:16:50.490 align:middle line:84%
and it should display the
RStudio running from the cloud.

00:16:50.490 --> 00:16:56.770 align:middle line:84%
And so here is a directory
in this cloud project

00:16:56.770 --> 00:17:01.570 align:middle line:84%
which has an RMD for
R Markdown file, which

00:17:01.570 --> 00:17:04.250 align:middle line:90%
is displayed in the top left.

00:17:04.250 --> 00:17:10.450 align:middle line:84%
And that simple program
creates that HTML file.

00:17:10.450 --> 00:17:15.589 align:middle line:84%
So analyses are very easy
to illustrate with RStudio.

00:17:15.589 --> 00:17:19.770 align:middle line:84%
And I hope to share
with you various R

00:17:19.770 --> 00:17:22.089 align:middle line:84%
programs that you can
use to replicate studies

00:17:22.089 --> 00:17:24.089 align:middle line:90%
we do in our lectures.

00:17:24.089 --> 00:17:25.589 align:middle line:90%
All right, let's see.

00:17:25.589 --> 00:17:30.670 align:middle line:84%
Finally, we distributed
assignment 0 today,

00:17:30.670 --> 00:17:35.690 align:middle line:84%
which is asking everyone to fill
out a simple survey of where you

00:17:35.690 --> 00:17:37.690 align:middle line:84%
come from, what
your interests are,

00:17:37.690 --> 00:17:41.170 align:middle line:84%
and to submit, perhaps,
a resume or CV if you

00:17:41.170 --> 00:17:42.430 align:middle line:90%
have that accessible.

00:17:42.430 --> 00:17:45.200 align:middle line:84%
So we can get to
know all of you.

00:17:45.200 --> 00:17:51.000 align:middle line:90%